-- title:
--   Potentials of a Gaussian kernel: time derivative and time-difference estimates
-- statement:
--   Let $S\subset\mathbb{R}^d$ and let $K$ be a kernel on $S$ with $x$-derivatives $K_1,K_2,K_3$ satisfying the Gaussian kernel hypotheses `KHyp` of the statement (Gaussian bounds $|\partial_x^jK(t,x,y)|\le A t^{-j/2}G_{ct}(x-y)$, measurability, the heat equation $\partial_tK=\frac12\Delta_xK$ and unit mass $\int_SK(t,x,y)dy=1$). For bounded measurable $h$ put $$k_j(t,x)=\int_S h(y)\,\partial_x^jK(t,x,y)\,dy .$$ If $h$ is bounded and $\mu$-Hölder on $S$ ($0\le\mu\le2$), then for $x\in S$, $t>0$, $0<s\le s'$: $$|k_0(t,x)-h(x)|\le A\,t^{\mu/2}H,\qquad\partial_tk_0=\tfrac12\operatorname{tr}k_2,\qquad k_0(s',x)-k_0(s,x)=\int_s^{s'}\tfrac12\operatorname{tr}k_2(\sigma,x)\,d\sigma,$$ and the corresponding time-difference bound for $k_1$.
--
--   **Formalization Note**: all objects are introduced by `let` in the statement; the statement is the conjunction of the listed facts, each in the exact form used later in the proof.
-- source:
--   A. Friedman, Partial Differential Equations of Parabolic Type, Prentice-Hall, 1964, Chapter 1 (initial values and time regularity of potentials); used in Stewart N. Ethier and Thomas G. Kurtz, Markov Processes: Characterization and Convergence, Wiley, 1986, https://doi.org/10.1002/9780470316658, Chapter 8, Section 1

